1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,050
Tools, materials, and gases.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,737
This is episode seven of From Sand to GPU.

3
00:00:06,737 --> 00:00:11,837
Every prior episode depends on an industrial base of equipment and materials.

4
00:00:11,837 --> 00:00:15,750
This layer explains why capacity cannot spike overnight.

5
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:18,162
Tools have long lead times.

6
00:00:18,162 --> 00:00:21,712
Chemistries are qualified per process recipe.

7
00:00:21,712 --> 00:00:25,312
Specialty gases need purity and logistics.

8
00:00:25,312 --> 00:00:28,075
Start with equipment chokepoints.

9
00:00:28,075 --> 00:00:41,487
Lithography is dominated in practice by ASML extreme-ultraviolet scanners, with Zeiss SMT optics a high-dependency link — ASML’s EUV position is confirmed as a practical monopoly; Zeiss dependency is likely high.

10
00:00:41,487 --> 00:00:50,287
Deposition, etch, and CMP sit with Applied Materials, Lam Research, Tokyo Electron, and ASM — oligopolies by segment, likely.

11
00:00:50,287 --> 00:00:56,287
Metrology is often described as KLA-centric; share figures are estimated unless primary.

12
00:00:56,287 --> 00:01:02,700
Packaging bonders from firms such as ASMPT and BESI matter when CoWoS-class capacity ramps.

13
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:08,175
Cleanrooms, ultrapure water, and abatement make fab CapEx a multi-year calendar.

14
00:01:08,175 --> 00:01:10,862
Materials are equally binding.

15
00:01:10,862 --> 00:01:19,875
Photoresists for advanced lithography are Japan-centric among suppliers such as JSR, TOK, Shin-Etsu, Fujifilm, and Sumitomo — likely.

16
00:01:19,875 --> 00:01:23,437
Photomask blanks involve Shin-Etsu and others.

17
00:01:23,437 --> 00:01:27,300
CMP slurries and pads are specialty chemistries.

18
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:31,525
ALD and CVD precursors are qualified node by node.

19
00:01:31,525 --> 00:01:39,725
ABF film for advanced substrates is Ajinomoto’s confirmed product role via company IR. Solder and underfill have multiple suppliers.

20
00:01:39,725 --> 00:01:43,925
Specialty gases run through front-end and back-end recipes.

21
00:01:43,925 --> 00:01:58,250
Illustrative examples — not a full bill of materials — include ultra-high-purity nitrogen, argon, and helium; hydrogen and oxygen; and process gases such as NF3, CF4, C4F8, chlorine, HBr, and WF6.

22
00:01:58,250 --> 00:02:03,375
Bulk and cylinder logistics matter; some fabs generate nitrogen on site.

23
00:02:03,375 --> 00:02:10,375
Regional majors such as Air Liquide, Linde, and Air Products are likely suppliers; fab contracts are rarely public.

24
00:02:10,375 --> 00:02:12,737
Risk notes stay honest.

25
00:02:12,737 --> 00:02:22,237
A single resist-plant outage can ripple across advanced nodes — historical event narratives exist, but specific percent-impact claims are estimated unless primary.

26
00:02:22,237 --> 00:02:26,250
Helium supply shocks intermittently affect some tools.

27
00:02:26,250 --> 00:02:30,525
Export controls on tools matter as much as controls on chips.

28
00:02:30,525 --> 00:02:38,137
The full qualified materials list for a specific TSMC node plus CoWoS-L flow used on Rubin is unknown — proprietary.

29
00:02:38,137 --> 00:02:41,262
How does this feed an AI GPU ramp?

30
00:02:41,262 --> 00:02:54,862
Simultaneously four ways: wafer-start limited by EUV and foundry tools; package limited by CoWoS tools and HBM; materials limited by resist, ABF, and gases; and system limited by power, cooling, and ODM capacity.

31
00:02:54,862 --> 00:02:59,350
Equipment and materials are the CapEx calendar behind those limits.

32
00:02:59,350 --> 00:03:15,687
Trackers should watch ASML shipment and backlog commentary from primary IR, wafer-fab-equipment spend forecasts from SEMI and vendors, resist or specialty-chem force-majeure notices, industrial-gas commentary including helium, and CapEx guides from TSMC, Samsung, and Intel.

33
00:03:15,687 --> 00:03:24,600
OECD and industry bottleneck atlases are useful checklists for concentration risk, but quantify only after validating against primary filings.

34
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:29,425
That is episode seven: the invisible stack under sand-to-GPU.

35
00:03:29,425 --> 00:03:35,887
Next time: Meet Vera Rubin — NVIDIA’s rack-scale platform, using only confirmed public figures.

